  "response": "## One correction to the scan first\n\n`crimes` reports crime_00002 (`mixed_utc_local_methods`) as **\"1 receiver `d`\" spanning L9–L41**. There are actually two distinct `d` bindings in two function scopes, and the detector merged them by identifier name:\n\n- `todayKey()` — `const d` at L8, read at L9: `getUTCFullYear()` / `getUTCMonth()` / `getUTCDate()`. **All UTC. Consistent.**\n- `dateLabel()` — `const d` at L40, read at L41: `getUTCFullYear()` / `getMonth()` / `getDate()`. **Mixed.**\n\nThe charge is real, but it lives entirely inside `dateLabel`. L9 is a false half of the pair.\n\n## Fix first: `dateLabel()` (src/date.ts:39-42)\n\n**Receiver whose methods disagree: `d`**, declared at `src/date.ts:40`.\n- UTC family: `d.getUTCFullYear()`\n- Local family: `d.getMonth()`, `d.getDate()`\n\n### Why this one, and why it's worse than \"off by an offset\"\n\nThe two families don't just shift the value — they can produce a date that never existed. Host at `UTC-5`, wall clock `2026-12-31 19:30` (= `2027-01-01 00:30Z`):\n\n| call | value |\n|---|---|\n| `d.getUTCFullYear()` | `2027` |\n| `d.getMonth()` | `11` |\n| `d.getDate()` | `31` |\n\n→ `\"2027-11-31\"`. Wrong year, and November has 30 days. Anything grouping or sorting on this label silently buckets the record into a nonexistent day, and only for hosts west of UTC during a ~5-hour window. Two other bugs ride along in the same expression: `getMonth()` is 0-indexed (December renders as `11`), and nothing is zero-padded, so labels sort lexically wrong (`\"2026-9-2\"` vs `\"2026-10-1\"`).\n\n### Proposed refactor — pick UTC, and let the formatter do the assembling\n\n`dateLabel` returns a machine-shaped `YYYY-MM-DD` string, not human-facing prose, so UTC is the right family: the label should mean the same thing on every host. The whole expression collapses to one call:\n\n```ts\nexport function dateLabel(now: Date = new Date()): string {\n  return now.toISOString().slice(0, 10); // YYYY-MM-DD, UTC\n}\n```\n\nThat single change closes three findings at once — crime_00002 (`mixed_utc_local_methods`, high), crime_00021 (`date_string_concat`, L41), and part of crime_00001 (`direct_date`, L40) via the injectable `now` parameter. `toISOString()` is UTC by definition, so there is no second family left to disagree with, and it pads and 1-indexes for free.\n\n**If instead the label is meant to be the viewer's local day** (the scan can't tell you this — no caller in the repo references `dateLabel`), the same principle applies from the other side: commit to local for all three reads and build with an explicit formatter rather than `+`:\n\n```ts\nexport function dateLabel(now: Date = new Date()): string {\n  return new Intl.DateTimeFormat(\"en-CA\", { timeZone: \"UTC\" /* or the user's zone */ })\n    .format(now); // en-CA yields YYYY-MM-DD\n}\n```\n\nEither way the rule holds: **one family per receiver, converted once at the boundary.**\n\n### Notes on sequencing\n\n- `dateLabel` and `todayKey` become the same function once fixed — `todayKey` already has the UTC family right, it just shares the 0-index and padding bugs (`\"2026-9-22\"`). Fold the second into the first after the first lands, rather than in the same change.\n- Neither function has an in-repo caller (`grep` across the fixture: only the declarations). Blast radius is genuinely 0, which matches the scan's `blast_radius: 0` and makes this a safe first move.\n- Leave crime_00003 (`timezone_unsafe_parse`, L29/L33) and crime_00004 (`dst_naive_arithmetic`, L52) for separate changes — they're different decisions (what zone did the literal's author mean; which calendar library) and shouldn't ride on this one.\n\nI haven't edited anything — say the word and I'll apply the UTC version, or the local version if that's the intended reading.",
